## ## Conclusion

The dataset covers 221 post-2010 petrol cars sold in Delhi across 14 brands, sourced from ARAI, SIAM, and Autocar India. Key EDA findings:

- E20 Compatibility is the dominant factor separating cars into two clear mileage-drop bands, with Launch Year and Engine CC providing secondary adjustments within each band.
- E20_Drop_% behaves as a discrete, rule-derived value rather than continuous real-world noise — explaining why tree-based models (Random Forest) outperform linear models on this data.
- Real-world tested mileage is consistently lower than claimed/ARAI-certified mileage, a well-known claimed-vs-real gap in the Indian auto market.
- Newer cars show comparatively lower E20 drop within the same compatibility group, reflecting better fuel injection and E20-ready components in recent models.
- No major data quality issues remain after cleaning — minimal missing values, consistent categorical labels, and no extreme outliers requiring removal.

These findings directly informed the modeling choice (Random Forest over Linear Regression) and the feature set (Engine CC, Launch Year, E20 Compatible) used in the prediction pipeline.
